const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between a living will and a healthcare power of attorney?',
    answer: 'A living will is a legal document that explicitly details your personal wishes regarding specific medical treatments (such as mechanical ventilation, cardiopulmonary resuscitation, or artificial nutrition and hydration) when you are terminally ill or permanently unconscious. In contrast, a healthcare power of attorney (also called a healthcare proxy or medical surrogate designation) legally appoints a specific person you trust to make medical treatment decisions on your behalf whenever you are temporarily or permanently unable to speak for yourself.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a POLST and how is it different from an advance directive?',
    answer: 'A POLST (Portable Medical Order, also known as POST, MOLST, or MOST depending on your state) is an actionable medical order signed by a licensed physician, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant. While an advance directive expresses your general legal wishes for future care, a POLST translates those wishes into immediate, standing medical commands printed on bright paper that emergency medical technicians (EMTs) and paramedics are legally obligated to follow in your home or care facility.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does my advance directive from one state work if I am hospitalized in another state?',
    answer: 'Most states have statutory reciprocity laws that recognize valid, out-of-state advance directives as long as the document complied with the legal execution requirements of the state where it was created. However, because legal definitions and witness rules differ across state lines, medical providers in a different state may hesitate or request clarification. If you spend significant time residing in two states (such as snowbirds), completing an advance directive statutory form for both states is strongly recommended.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who cannot serve as a signing witness for my advance directive?',
    answer: 'To prevent financial and legal conflicts of interest, state laws strictly limit who can serve as an official signing witness. In most jurisdictions, you CANNOT use your designated healthcare agent or proxy, your treating doctor or nurses, employees of the hospital or nursing home where you receive care, or anyone named as a beneficiary in your primary estate will or trust. Ideal witnesses are neutral neighbors, friends, or a licensed Notary Public.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does an advance directive ever expire?',
    answer: 'No, a properly executed advance directive does not expire automatically over time. It remains legally binding and valid indefinitely until you formally revoke it, replace it with an updated version, or include a specific expiration date within the document itself. However, elder law attorneys recommend reviewing your advance directive every three to five years, or after major life events such as a diagnosis, marriage, divorce, or death of a proxy.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Where should I store my advance directive so doctors can find it in an emergency?',
    answer: 'You should keep your original signed document in an easily accessible, unlocked location at home (such as a desk drawer or bedside table) and give complete copies to your designated healthcare agent, alternate proxy, primary care physician, and local hospital system to upload into your electronic health record (EHR). NEVER lock your advance directive in a bank safe deposit box, because banks are closed during night and weekend medical emergencies.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I change or cancel my advance directive after I sign it?',
    answer: 'Yes. As long as you retain mental decision-making capacity, you have the legal right to revoke or amend your advance directive at any time. To cancel an old directive, physically destroy old copies, complete a fresh state statutory form, sign it according to state witness rules, and immediately provide updated copies to your healthcare agent, family doctor, and local hospital network.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I have no advance directive and become unable to communicate?',
    answer: 'If you become incapacitated without an advance directive, doctors must rely on your state\'s statutory "family consent hierarchy" laws to determine who can make choices for you—typically your legal spouse first, followed by adult children, parents, and siblings. If family members disagree on your medical treatment, medical teams may be forced to initiate expensive and emotionally draining court guardianship proceedings to appoint a legal guardian.',
  },
];
