      Advance Directive &amp; Healthcare Power of Attorney Guide — Free State Forms &amp; Requirements
    </h1>
    <p class="adg-date">State statutory requirements, legal execution rules &amp; forms last verified in {lastReviewed}.</p>

    <div class="adg-aeo-block" role="note" aria-label="Quick Answer">
      <p class="adg-aeo-label">Quick Answer</p>
      <p class="adg-aeo-text">
        An <strong class="adg-gold">Advance Directive</strong> is a legal framework combining a <strong class="adg-gold">Living Will</strong> (written treatment instructions for terminal illness) and a <strong class="adg-gold">Healthcare Power of Attorney</strong> (appointing a proxy to make medical choices if you are incapacitated). State laws govern whether you need two neutral witnesses or a Notary Public. Official statutory forms for all 50 states are legally binding without hiring an attorney or paying legal fees.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="adg-problem">
      Fewer than 40 percent of American seniors have completed an advance directive or designated a medical healthcare proxy. When unexpected medical emergencies or severe illnesses occur, hospital intensive care teams are left without clear legal guidance on your treatment preferences. In many states, this forces medical teams to follow rigid statutory family hierarchies or seek emergency court guardianship orders—creating severe emotional conflict among adult children and potential unwanted invasive medical interventions. This guide provides comprehensive, state-by-state statutory rules, execution requirements, and direct access to free official forms so you can protect your medical autonomy cleanly.
    </p>

    <ul class="adg-who-list" aria-label="Who this tool is for">
      <li class="adg-who-item"><span class="adg-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Seniors 60 and older who want to document their precise medical choices for end-of-life care and appoint a trusted family member as their legal healthcare advocate.</li>
      <li class="adg-who-item"><span class="adg-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Adult children and family caregivers assisting aging parents who are preparing for planned surgical procedures, hospital stays, or residential care facility admissions.</li>
      <li class="adg-who-item"><span class="adg-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Individuals structuring comprehensive estate and healthcare protection alongside our <a href="/tools/simple-will-estate-basics-explainer/" class="adg-inline-link">Simple Will &amp; Estate Basics Explainer</a> and <a href="/tools/family-trusted-contact-setup-guide/" class="adg-inline-link">Family Trusted Contact Setup Guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
