  <!-- ELEMENT 4 — HOW TO USE -->
  <section class="adg-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-heading">
    <h2 id="how-to-heading">How to Complete Your State Advance Directive &amp; Medical Proxy — Step by Step</h2>
    
    <p class="adg-intro-p">
      Executing a legally binding advance directive does not require expensive legal representation. Every state provides official statutory language that becomes legally enforceable as long as you follow your state's specific execution protocol. Follow these four steps to ensure your medical rights are legally protected across all health systems.
    </p>

    <ol class="adg-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Step 1 — Select a Primary Healthcare Agent and an Alternate Proxy.</strong>
        Choose a calm, emotionally resilient adult (such as a spouse, adult child, or trusted relative) who shares your medical values and understands your personal philosophy on quality of life. Always appoint an alternate agent in your document so that if your primary surrogate is traveling, ill, or unreachable during a critical hospital event, your secondary agent can step in without court delay.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 2 — Document Your Explicit Treatment Instructions (Living Will).</strong>
        Be specific about the circumstances under which you accept or decline aggressive life-prolonging treatments. Address key medical interventions including mechanical ventilation (breathing machines), cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), artificial nutrition and hydration (feeding tubes and IV fluids), kidney dialysis, experimental therapies, and organ donation preferences. Specify your desire for comfort-focused palliative and hospice care.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 3 — Execute the Document According to Your State's Statutory Witness Rules.</strong>
        State laws mandate strict execution rituals to prove you were of sound mind and acting voluntarily. Most states require your signature to be witnessed by two qualified adults who are completely disinterested—meaning they cannot be your healthcare proxy, your treating doctor, an employee of your care facility, or an heir to your estate. Alternatively, many states allow execution before a licensed Notary Public instead of witnesses.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 4 — Distribute Legible Copies to Your Medical Network.</strong>
        Provide signed copies to your primary healthcare agent, alternate surrogate, primary care physician, and local hospital network to upload directly into your Electronic Health Record (EHR). Keep the original document in an easily accessible, unlocked place at home (such as a bedroom nightstand or refrigerator folder). Never store an advance directive in a bank safe deposit box, as banks are closed during emergency hospital admissions.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="adg-pro-tip">
      <p class="adg-tip-label">💡 Pro Tip for Frail or Chronically Ill Seniors</p>
      <p class="adg-tip-text">
        If you have a serious medical condition, advanced frailty, or a life-limiting diagnosis, ask your primary care physician about completing a <strong>POLST (Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment)</strong> or <strong>MOLST</strong> in addition to your advance directive. While an advance directive is a legal instrument expressing general wishes for future care, a POLST is a bright-colored, doctor-signed medical order that translates your directive into immediate, actionable standing commands. Emergency medical responders (EMTs and paramedics) are legally required to honor a POLST in your home, whereas they cannot interpret legal living wills in emergency field situations.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Terminology Table -->
    <div class="adg-table-section">
      <h3>Comprehensive Medical Planning Terms &amp; Abbreviations Explained</h3>
      <p class="adg-table-intro">
        Medical and legal documentation often uses confusing acronyms and legal phrasing. Review this reference table to understand the exact definitions of common terms used in advance care planning.
      </p>
      <div class="adg-table-wrap">
        <table class="adg-ref-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Term / Abbreviation</th><th>Full Official Name</th><th>Detailed Plain-English Definition</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Healthcare POA</strong></td>
              <td>Healthcare Power of Attorney / Medical Proxy</td>
              <td>A legal document designating a specific surrogate (agent) with the legal authority to make medical decisions for you if an injury or illness leaves you temporarily or permanently unable to make or communicate decisions yourself.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="adg-row-alt">
              <td><strong>Living Will</strong></td>
              <td>Directive to Physicians / Medical Declaration</td>
              <td>A legal instrument detailing your explicit written instructions regarding life-sustaining medical interventions (such as ventilators, feeding tubes, and dialysis) when you are in a terminal condition or persistent vegetative state.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>POLST / MOLST</strong></td>
              <td>Portable Medical Order / Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment</td>
              <td>A actionable, doctor-signed medical order printed on bright pink or green paper that instructs emergency medical technicians (EMTs) on whether to initiate CPR, intubation, or transport during an acute home crisis.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="adg-row-alt">
              <td><strong>DNR</strong></td>
              <td>Do Not Resuscitate Order</td>
              <td>A specific medical order issued by a physician directing healthcare staff and emergency responders not to perform cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) or chest compressions if your breathing or heartbeat stops.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>DNI</strong></td>
              <td>Do Not Intubate Order</td>
              <td>A medical instruction specifying that medical staff should not insert a breathing tube down your throat or connect you to a mechanical ventilator machine during respiratory failure.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="adg-row-alt">
              <td><strong>Healthcare Proxy</strong></td>
              <td>Designated Medical Surrogate / Agent</td>
              <td>The trusted individual named in your healthcare power of attorney who is legally empowered to consult with physicians, inspect medical records, and authorize or decline medical treatments on your behalf.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Capacity</strong></td>
              <td>Decision-Making Capacity</td>
              <td>The clinical assessment made by attending physicians evaluating whether a patient has sufficient mental ability to understand medical information, weigh risks and benefits, and communicate a voluntary medical choice.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="adg-row-alt">
              <td><strong>Palliative Care</strong></td>
              <td>Specialized Comfort Medical Care</td>
              <td>Medical care focused on providing relief from the symptoms, pain, and physical/emotional stress of a serious illness, available alongside curative treatment at any stage of illness.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

