          <tr class="adg-row-alt"><td>Living Will Clause</td><td>Explicitly stated refusal of prolonged mechanical life support if brain recovery was clinically deemed improbable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Medical Team Action</td><td class="adg-yes">✅ Hospital ethics committee and attending ICU physicians honored written directive directly</td></tr>
          <tr class="adg-row-alt"><td>Family Resolution</td><td class="adg-yes">✅ Legal authority granted to daughter; family avoided expensive court guardianship litigation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="adg-result-block">
        <span class="adg-result-highlight">✅ Written Directive Prevented Court Battle &amp; Enforced Margaret's Personal Values</span>
        Because Margaret had previously executed a valid statutory healthcare proxy and living will, her daughter had undisputed legal authority to guide the medical team. The ICU staff transitioned Margaret to comfort-focused palliative care according to her written wishes, preventing prolonged pain and family conflict.
      </div>
      <p class="adg-action">
        <strong>Takeaway:</strong> Executing a statutory advance directive eliminates guesswork for your children and ensures your personal healthcare philosophy is legally binding. Explore estate planning fundamentals with our <a href="/tools/simple-will-estate-basics-explainer/" class="adg-inline-link">Simple Will &amp; Estate Basics Explainer</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="adg-scenario-card">
      <div class="adg-scenario-header">
        <span class="adg-tag">Scenario 2</span>
        <strong class="adg-scenario-name">Robert, Age 82 — POLST Medical Order Honors Home Care Wishes During Emergency</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="adg-scenario-desc">
        Robert has end-stage chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and heart failure. He resides at home and wished to avoid traumatic hospital intubations or intensive care unit admissions.
      </p>
      <table class="adg-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Planning Factor</th><th>Details &amp; Execution Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Document Completed</td><td>State POLST Form (Do Not Resuscitate + Comfort-Focused Medical Interventions) signed by physician</td></tr>
          <tr class="adg-row-alt"><td>Emergency Event</td><td>Neighbor called 911 when Robert experienced acute breathing distress</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EMS Field Action</td><td class="adg-yes">✅ Paramedics located bright POLST on refrigerator; provided nebulizer treatments &amp; oxygen without intubation</td></tr>
          <tr class="adg-row-alt"><td>Outcome</td><td class="adg-yes">✅ Transferred smoothly to home hospice care as requested; avoided invasive ICU ventilator setup</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="adg-result-block">
        <span class="adg-result-highlight">✅ Actionable POLST Order Respected by EMTs — Prevented Unwanted Traumatic Resuscitation</span>
        Under state law, paramedics responding to a 911 call must initiate CPR and aggressive resuscitation unless an official, doctor-signed POLST or DNR order is physically presented immediately. Robert's bright POLST order ensured his home hospice preferences were legally enforced in the field.
      </div>
      <p class="adg-action">
        <strong>Takeaway:</strong> If you suffer from advanced chronic illness, ask your doctor to sign a POLST order to complement your standard advance directive.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="adg-disclaimer">
      These representative scenarios illustrate typical legal and medical outcomes under state statutory rules. Individual results vary based on local laws, hospital policies, and medical diagnoses.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ELEMENT 6 — PITFALLS -->
  <section class="adg-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Advance Directive Mistakes — And How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Locking Original Documents in a Bank Safe Deposit Box</h3>
    <p>
      A common mistake made by seniors is placing their newly signed advance directive inside a bank safe deposit box. In an acute medical emergency occurring on a weekend or night, family members cannot access the bank box, leaving hospital doctors unaware of your wishes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to keep your original document in an accessible, unlocked place at home (such as a bedroom drawer) and immediately provide digital or paper copies to your designated proxy, primary care physician, and local hospital network.
    </p>

    <h3>Using Disqualified Witnesses During the Signing Ceremony</h3>
    <p>
      State laws strictly invalidate advance directives if they are signed by witnesses who have a conflict of interest. Using your appointed proxy, treating physician, hospital nurse, or a family member listed in your will as a witness can render the entire document legally void.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to sign your advance directive before two neutral neighbors or co-workers who have zero financial interest in your estate, or execute the document before an official Notary Public.
    </p>

    <h3>Assuming a Financial Power of Attorney Covers Healthcare Decisions</h3>
    <p>
      Many seniors assume that executing a general financial Power of Attorney gives their adult children the legal right to make hospital treatment choices. Under federal HIPAA privacy laws and state medical statutes, a financial POA grants zero medical authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to execute a separate Healthcare Power of Attorney / Medical Proxy alongside your financial documents. Setup account safety contacts using our <a href="/tools/family-trusted-contact-setup-guide/" class="adg-inline-link">Family Trusted Contact Setup Guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Failing to Update Documents After Major Life Changes</h3>
    <p>
      An advance directive executed 20 years ago may name an ex-spouse, a deceased relative, or reflect medical treatment views that no longer align with your current health situation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to review your advance directive every three to five years and formally revoke old documents whenever you complete an updated state statutory form.
    </p>
  </section>

